From b5ced2725d870d27491e5da0d0819d7c3cc2eb22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 06:31:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(admission): bounds-check ZIP64 locator offset and
 saturate part cap

Two integer overflows in input admission could panic on untrusted input
(debug: overflow panic; release: wrapped index, then a slice panic).

1. `declared_entry_count` read the ZIP64 locator's 8-byte record offset
   from attacker-controlled bytes and guarded it with `r + 40 <=
   bytes.len()`. On 64-bit `usize::try_from(u64)` always succeeds, so an
   offset near `usize::MAX` overflowed the addition before the guard ran.
   The end is now `checked_add`, the record is taken with `bytes.get`,
   and the count is read from that 40-byte slice; an offset that does
   not fit the buffer is `INVALID_PACKAGE` ("ZIP64 end record missing").

2. `admit` wrapped each entry in `take(cap + 1)` with `cap =
   max_part_bytes.min(remaining)`; a caller setting both
   `max_part_bytes` and `max_uncompressed_bytes` to `u64::MAX` overflowed
   it. The cap saturates.

Tests in `admission::tests` build the ZIP64 tails by hand from the
`zip` crate's output: a locator past the end, locators at `u64::MAX`,
`u64::MAX - 20`, `u64::MAX - 39` and `usize::MAX - 39`, a record
truncated by the buffer, a valid ZIP64 record (count still read), and
a docx admitted under unbounded part and package budgets. Valid
archives behave as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
